---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Estate Tax Atlas 2026: who publishes these calculators';
const description = "Estate Tax Atlas is published by Radif Partners: 2026 state estate and inheritance taxes read on each state's law and computed on your estate. Nothing sold.";
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Estate Tax Atlas</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>The gap this site fills</h2>
    <p>Search for "state estate tax" and the first result is a table dated 2018 that still shows a federal exemption of $11.2 million. The next ones are charts for 2025, with Washington's old exemption and New York missing, or a 2026 article that gives Connecticut's exemption as $13.6 million when the state's law ties it to the federal amount, {K.usd(K.E.CT.exemption)} this year. Most of these pages end with a form to book a meeting with an adviser. None computes a tax.</p>
    <p>This site does better than the first Google result because it computes the state estate tax, the federal estate tax and the inheritance tax paid by each heir on the estate you enter, with the 2026 exemptions and tables of the 13 estate tax jurisdictions and the 5 inheritance tax states read one by one on the state's law or revenue department, where the page ranked first dates from 2018 and still shows a federal exemption of $11.2 million.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public rules. It is not a law firm, an estate planner, a trust company, a bank or an insurer; it is not run by or affiliated with the IRS, any state revenue department or any government agency; it receives no commission and has no partner to send you to.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we rely on</h2>
    <p>For the federal tax: Public Law 119-21 (<Fragment set:html={src('pl_119_21', 'govinfo.gov')} />), Rev. Proc. 2025-32 for the 2026 amounts and the Form 706 instructions. For each state, the statute and the revenue department page or form listed on its page, for example Tax Law section 952 and the ET-706 instructions in New York, Form MET 1 in Maryland, the Attorney General's calculator notes in Illinois, ESB 6347 and the Department of Revenue tables in Washington, and KRS chapter 140 as amended in 2026 in Kentucky. All were read on {K.day(K.P.retrieved_at)}.</p>
    <h2>What the site leaves out</h2>
    <p>The value of the estate is yours to enter: appraisals, the valuation of a business or of a farm, and special-use valuation are not modelled. Trust drafting, probate procedure and generation-skipping transfer tax are not covered. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every rule, formula and simplification; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for corrections and sources. We answer questions about a rule, not about your own estate.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
